    {
      "source": "Doctors of Running",
      "priority": 3,
      "url": "https://www.doctorsofrunning.com/2024/10/adidas-adizero-adios-pro-4-review-2024.html",
      "type": "qualitative",
      "keyFindings": [
        "Cushioning: The new Lightstrike Pro is the softest and bounciest Adidas version yet (moderately soft but very resilient), with smooth heel transition and comfortable forefoot landings on a wider front platform.",
        "Responsiveness: Energy return is a standout—reviewers describe it as extremely responsive and fast, with strong bounce plus an early forefoot rocker for quick, snappy toe-off.",
        "Stability: Stability is mixed; the shoe is stiff and rearfoot lockdown is decent despite no heel counter, but the medial midfoot cutout can feel unstable (pronation/collapse), especially at slower paces or downhill.",
        "Durability: Durability is rated above average, with reviewers calling it durable overall, citing a grippy outsole and upper materials that hold up well under force."
      ]
    },
    {
      "source": "Road Trail Run",
      "priority": 4,
      "url": "https://www.roadtrailrun.com/2024/10/adidas-adizero-adios-pro-4-multi-tester.html",
      "type": "qualitative",
      "keyFindings": [
        "Cushioning: The Lightstrike Pro midsole is tuned slightly softer and bouncier than Adios Pro 3, with unchanged 39 mm heel / 33 mm forefoot stack giving strong marathon-level impact protection and a smoother heel-to-forefoot roll.",
        "Responsiveness: Testers describe energy return from Lightstrike Pro plus composite rods as world-class, with a longer rocker and strong toe-off pop, though the softer setup can feel a bit less sharply snappy for some runners.",
        "Stability: Stability is mixed; the rear-to-midfoot is notably rigid, but the softer foam and more compliant heel counter/upper were reported to allow more medial-lateral roll, so lateral support feels reduced versus Pro 3.",
        "Durability: Long-term durability data is limited in this review, but the new Lightraxion outsole keeps Continental rubber in the high-wear toe area, indicating targeted reinforcement where outsole abrasion is highest."
      ]
    },
    {
      "source": "Believe in the Run",
      "priority": 5,
      "url": "https://believeintherun.com/shoe-reviews/adidas-adizero-adios-pro-4-review/",
      "type": "qualitative",
      "keyFindings": [
        "Cushioning: The new Lightstrike Pro is described as softer and bouncier than the Adios Pro 3, and the 39 mm heel / 33 mm forefoot stack points to strong long-distance impact protection with a balanced 6 mm drop feel.",
        "Responsiveness: The ride is called smoother, quicker, and bouncier, while the Evo Pro-inspired EnergyRods (60% fulcrum point) are designed to improve leverage and snap through toe-off.",
        "Stability: The woven mesh is built to stretch vertically while holding the foot laterally, and the internal Lightlock band wrapping from heel around the midfoot adds containment and lateral support; the rod structure also implies higher torsional rigidity.",
        "Durability: The outsole uses a thick Continental rubber patch in the key medial forefoot landing zone plus strategically placed new-compound grip sections, indicating targeted wear resistance, though no long-term wear results are given in the excerpt."
      ]
    }
